package org.mattshoe.mtg.core

internal val setDuskmourn = StorySection(
    "dsk", "Duskmourn: House of Horror",
    "A lonely girl made friends with the thing in her basement, and now the whole world is one endless house that feeds on fear.",
    listOf(
        StoryArticle(
            "dsk-the-house", "The House",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "dsk-welcome-home", "Welcome Home",
                    listOf(
                        "House of Horror came out on September 27, 2024, under the set code DSK. It was the hundred and second expansion in the game's history, and it came straight after Bloomburrow, a set of brave little animals in sunlit meadows, which made the change of tone all the sharper. Its story sits in the Omenpath Era, after the Phyrexian invasion had left the doors between worlds hanging open, and it is the second set of the story arc that builds toward [Tarkir Dragonstorm](tarkir-dragonstorm). Its tagline told players to face their fears, all of them.",
                        "The set is a love letter to modern horror, the slasher films, haunted houses and creepy suburban nightmares of the seventies, eighties and nineties. The world it shows looks almost like ours. There are television sets crackling with static, telephones, chainsaws, videotapes, sneakers and flashlights. There is a funfair gone to rot, a cornfield that rustles with no wind, and a cellar door nobody should open. The whole of it takes place inside one building, because on this plane there is nothing else left.",
                        "That building is the House. It is not a haunted mansion on a hill above a town. It is the town, and the city beyond it, and the forests and seas and both suns, swallowed and folded into one endless run of rooms. People who live in it say the House is the world and the world is the House. The world it ate was once called something else, but that name is long forgotten. Everyone now calls it [Duskmourn](duskmourn).",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "Face Your Fears. All of Them.",
                        "Duskmourn: House of Horror tagline",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "dsk-the-ascension", "The Ascension",
                    listOf(
                        "Before the House, Duskmourn was a world of high magic and old wonders, with cities and coasts and mountains, and in its deep past dragons flew over its cities and burned them. Magic was scarce and held by a few, so its people learned to summon. Spirits from the plane's spirit realm lit their lamps and kept their food cold, and the plane grew clever and modern on borrowed power. It had three parts, a physical realm where people lived and two realms beyond it, one of spirits and one of demons.",
                        "In a middle-class neighbourhood, on a street corner near the woods, stood an older house with a black shingle roof, a gray brick front and gargoyles in its rafters. One of its many owners summoned a demon out of the demon realm, hoping for a simple household servant. What came was Valgavoth, and it tried to eat everything it could reach. The summoners could not banish it, but the crossing had weakened it, and they bound it to the structure of the house itself and buried it beneath the basement. They swore to keep watch. Then they died, the house passed through a dozen hands, and the thing below waited.",
                        "The Vendrell family bought the house decades before the story begins, and their lonely daughter Marina found the presence in the cellar. After she brought it four victims, the house began to grow, and the growth is remembered as the Ascension. It took only a handful of years. The homes on either side went first, then the city, then the other cities, along the roads like blood along arteries. It swallowed the greater sun, then the last wild places, and the elves' Rotrue Wood was the final forest to fall. The lesser sun followed. The demon realm was folded into the House, and the spirit realm was pushed out to the edges where the plane meets the [Blind Eternities](the-blind-eternities).",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "Valgavoth whispered into Marina's ear until he was all she could hear.",
                        "Demonic Counsel",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "dsk-the-five-zones", "The Five Zones",
                    listOf(
                        "The House is infinite, but it is not shapeless. It is divided into five great zones, each a run of rooms with its own weather and its own way of killing, and each one watched over by a towering Overlord. The rooms shift all the time, and they shift most when nobody is looking. Some look like proper rooms. Others look like the outdoors, a meadow or a lake or a forest, but there is always a door or a window somewhere that leads on to the next room.",
                        "The Mistmoors are the white zone, rooms of pale stone and billowing drapes and drifts of windblown sand under flat gray skies with no sun. They are vast and silent, full of attics, foyers, patios and chapels, and they hold part of the Valley of Serenity, the pastures where the cult keeps its village. The Floodpits are the blue zone, all water in every form, stained ceilings and walls of staticky screens, the most impossible geometry in the House. Their reflections make them thick with ghosts. The Balemurk is the black zone, rotten boards, septic marshes, crypts and catacombs, and at its bottom lies the basement of the original house and the tunnel down to the Below, where Valgavoth lives.",
                        "The Boilerbilges are the red zone, the hardest place in the House to stay alive. They are kitchens and furnace rooms and dining halls full of sheer drops, jagged architecture, belching flames, earthquakes and twisters of fire, and they hold the razor mazes where the razorkin build their traps. The Hauntwoods are the green zone, where inside and outside blur. Hand-shaped trees hold up the ceilings, brambles and carnivorous plants creep through gyms, greenhouses and conservatories, and a hedge maze winds away into the dark. The rare land cycle called the verges, {Floodfarm Verge} among them, shows these zones bleeding into one another at their edges.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "It is the one chamber whose location never changes, the rotten, rancorous heart of Duskmourn itself.",
                        "Valgavoth's Lair",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "dsk-harrowings", "Harrowings",
                    listOf(
                        "The House has seasons, and they follow the demon's body. Valgavoth molts, shedding one form and growing into the next. In the weeks before a molt his attention turns inward, and the House falls into a lull called a Quiescence, which is as close to safety as anyone inside ever gets. Survivors use those weeks to rest, scavenge and get ready. Then the molt comes, and with it a Harrowing, a few days when his malice surges through every wall. A fresh wave of cellarspawn is pushed out into the rooms, and cultists, razorkin and every other monster in the House turn more vicious and paranoid than before.",
                        "Moths are everywhere. They are Valgavoth's sign, and the eyespots on their wings are the eyes of the House. If you can see a moth, the House can see you. Windows serve the same purpose. Survivors learn to step around the moth patterns woven into old carpets, to keep quiet, and to never trust a room that feels safe. The ones who last name their children after things the world has lost, City, Dawn, Sunset and Winter, words for places and times that none of them have ever seen.",
                        "For generations an uneasy truce held. The House let a few safe zones stand, often on the borders between zones, and it restocked food, packaging and all, as bait, so that the people it farmed would stay alive and keep being afraid. Opening a door to another world cost Valgavoth so much that he could manage one every few years. The [Omenpaths](the-omenpaths) changed that. Doors came cheaply now and new victims poured in from Kamigawa, Ravnica, Kaldheim and Arcavios, so the House no longer needed to farm anyone. It began to hunt, and the safe zones began to fall.",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Set", "Duskmourn: House of Horror"),
                StorySource("Story", "Planeswalker's Guide to Duskmourn, by Emily Teng, on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Story", "Duskmourn: House of Horror, Welcome Home and Dead End, by Mira Grant, Magic Story"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Duskmourn"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Duskmourn: House of Horror"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "dsk-the-rescue", "The Rescue",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "dsk-the-cherry-door", "The Cherry Door",
                    listOf(
                        "The story begins with a boy who would not let his mother go. Tamiyo, the moonfolk storyteller of Kamigawa, had been compleated and killed in the invasion, and an echo of her lived on in one of her own story scrolls. Her adopted son Nashi, a young nezumi, carried that scroll with him. Then it vanished, and Nashi told his friends among the Reckoners that he could hear her calling. Three months later he was gone, and four of those friends with him.",
                        "The Wanderer, now Kamigawa's emperor, had lost her spark in the [Desparkening](sparks-lost). She found Kaito Shizuki in the middle of robbing an imperial vault and asked him to bring the boy home. Kaito gathered a team. Tyvar Kell, the stone-skinned elf prince of Kaldheim, came for the glory of it and brought Niko Aris, a former planeswalker from Theros who still threw shards of light and was bitter about the spark they had lost. The trail led to Ravnica, to a quarantined courtyard where a lone door of stained cherry wood, carved with moths and branches, stood with nothing behind it. People had gone through and not come back.",
                        "Niv-Mizzet attached one of his researchers to the rescue, Zimone Wola of Strixhaven, a brilliant scholar of dimensions who thought the Omenpaths the beginning of a wonderful age. The detective Alquist Proft said flatly that the door bore none of the marks of an Omenpath. A young former planeswalker named Aminatou, who could twist fate, recoiled from it, because her magic and the door's pushed against each other like oil and water. She gave each of them a token of her power, enough to cheat one death. Then the door swung open on its own, onto a dusty foyer seen through a haze of blue light. {Enter the Enigma} shows the Wanderer crossing, and the door slammed behind the last of them.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "Though she was used to stepping into the unknown, something about this strange doorway made the Wanderer shiver as she crossed the threshold.",
                        "Enter the Enigma",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "dsk-dont-split-up", "Don't Split the Party",
                    listOf(
                        "Inside, the rooms would not stay put. Hallways changed behind them, a kitchen opened into a bedroom, and beyond a pair of glass doors an abandoned funfair loomed over a field of rotting corn. Niko led by always keeping to the right, a trick from the labyrinths of Theros. Then a scream drew Tyvar through a side door, Zimone ran after him, and the doorway behind them turned to wall. Kaito spun to follow and the floor opened under him like a lamprey's mouth, ringed with teeth, and swallowed him. Within minutes the House had taken three of the five, and the Wanderer was left on her knees before smooth tile.",
                        "Niko and the Wanderer pressed on through a cold room of hanging carcasses, and there a gaunt young man in clothes stitched from a dozen sources, some of them wallpaper, pulled them aside. He held up a note asking for silence, then brought down a masked razorkin with a slingshot and a bear trap. His name was Winter. He had lived in the House most of his life, he said, and he knew how to move through it and which things in it would not kill you. He offered to guide them, and with no better choice they let him.",
                        "Tyvar and Zimone came out in a vast library that held the lost knowledge of an entire world, and Zimone could hardly be dragged away from it. Tyvar, whose magic lets him take on the substance of whatever he touches, punched into the walls and learned to wrap the two of them in the House's own flesh, so that its monsters could not see them. Kaito fell through a burning forest and a dead city where dragons wheeled, an echo of Duskmourn's distant past, and landed in a basement, where he punched a man creeping up behind him.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "dsk-dont-look-back", "Don't Look Back",
                    listOf(
                        "The man was [Jace Beleren](who/jace), whom Kaito had last seen at the Invasion Tree. Jace had come to the House looking for Vraska and a small companion of theirs, both lost somewhere inside. The two planeswalkers fought their way down together. Jace unmade a nightmare by reaching into the minds trapped inside it and taking away their fear, because a nightmare cannot live without fear. Then the floor gave way into a pit of gel, and shackles closed on both their ankles and began to drag them under.",
                        "Kaito's drone picked Jace free first. Jace reached back to help, then looked up and saw, projected on the ceiling, Vraska shielding a small orange creature from a pack of razorkin. He apologised, said the creature was too important, and climbed, and every rung he touched dragged Kaito deeper. Kaito tore himself out of the House with his spark and landed back in the Ravnican alley, clutching a bloodstained splinter of wood. When he tried to go back, nothing happened. Aminatou told him his spark had not gone out. The House itself was refusing him.",
                        "Meanwhile Winter walked Niko and the Wanderer through a little parlor with a rolling, careful gait, stepping around every moth woven into the faded carpet. Do not step on the moths, he warned, because the House is alive and it hunts. It lures people in, splits them apart and sends its creatures after them one by one. He told them to follow the Wanderer's glimmer, a small glowing dragon spirit, a tiny Kyodai made from her hope, and they followed its light deeper in, toward the boy they had come to find.",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Set", "Duskmourn: House of Horror"),
                StorySource("Story", "Duskmourn: House of Horror, Episodes 1 to 3, by Mira Grant, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Duskmourn: House of Horror"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Winter"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "dsk-the-threshold", "The Threshold",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "dsk-dont-give-up", "Don't Give Up",
                    listOf(
                        "Following the glimmer, Winter, Niko and the Wanderer crossed rooms where glitch ghosts crawled out of mirrors and illusions shifted underfoot. Winter let slip that he was not from the House originally after all. In an outdoor room they found a clearing where wickerfolk, living figures of woven wood, held four nezumi captive, and one of them was Nashi. {Shardmage's Rescue} shows Niko shattering the wicker things and pulling him clear. They won by using the wickerfolk's fear of fire, and the Wanderer's fate token from Aminatou was spent in the fight.",
                        "The rescue did not end the quest, because Nashi would not leave. His mother was still somewhere in the House, he could still hear her, and he meant to find her. So the party went deeper, through room after room of trapped forest and thicket that no building should be able to hold, with Winter leading and the Wanderer watching him more closely with every turn.",
                        "The small orange creature Vraska had been shielding from the razorkin was named Loot, a strange child who could see where every Omenpath went. Loot was the reason [Jace](who/jace) had been willing to leave Kaito in the pit, and the reason Valgavoth wanted him too, because the demon's real goal was to learn the roads to every world in the Multiverse so that he could open his doors on all of them.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "dsk-dont-give-in", "Don't Give In",
                    listOf(
                        "Tyvar and Zimone wandered into the oldest rooms of the House, where everything turned clean and lit and ordinary, a well-kept mansion in soft light. A teenage girl in antique clothes walked through it as if nothing were wrong. She was Marina Vendrell, still a girl after all these decades, seeing the world as she remembered it. When they pressed her with the truth she grew frightened and denied it, and the House around her began to curdle back into horror. They took her diary. It told the whole story of Marina and Val, and Zimone understood what the rituals in its margins meant. With the power of four lives, Valgavoth could stretch his reach again and again until nothing at all was left outside him.",
                        "Winter led Niko, Nashi and the Wanderer into an impossibly broad meadow under an endless twilight, dotted with huts with smoke rising from their chimneys. This was the Valley of Serenity, and beneath it lay a cavern chapel hung with chrysalises. Robed cultists, clean and well fed where every other survivor was ragged, held three prisoners. One, a wounded forager named Rill, gave in and agreed to join them. Another was her friend Sunset.",
                        "The rescuers stepped in to stop it, and Winter bowed his head and sealed them in with the cultists. The cult's leader, the seneschal Victor, blew a cloud of silver moth dust from his book, and everyone fell. {Betrayer's Bargain} shows the deal Winter had made. Valgavoth honours the Rite of Four, a boon to anyone who offers him four lives, and Winter had promised him exactly that.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "This is my way out. You'll have to find your own.",
                        "Winter, to Niko, Betrayer's Bargain",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "dsk-dont-die", "Don't Die",
                    listOf(
                        "Niko woke tied to a chair before a quartz altar. The cultists opened a box, and from it rose the shade of Tamiyo, flickering and turned away. Victor explained the plan. The prisoners carried the dust of many worlds, and through them Valgavoth would find his way to every one. Then the demon came out of the dark above, an enormous moth spun out of nightmare, his wings grown into the walls. He had promised Winter freedom. Four lives, and there were only three prisoners, because the first had been paid long ago. It was Winter's best friend, the person he loved most, who had come through a door into the House with him when they were both teenagers.",
                        "Valgavoth opened a door to Innistrad for him. Before Winter could reach it, Tyvar came crashing through the cultists laughing, and Zimone, turned into a thing of splintered wood by Tyvar's disguise, cut Niko loose. The Wanderer dueled the demon on his own altar, and Niko sealed Winter away from his door inside a shard. A cultist drove a knife through Niko's eye, the world snapped back like a bowstring, and Aminatou's token was spent. Tamiyo spoke to her son. The cult had stolen almost every story she was made of, and only her love for him was left, which they had used as bait. She asked him to let her go.",
                        "Valgavoth pinned the Wanderer and opened his mouth over her. Nashi pulled the scroll free, Tamiyo smiled and dissolved into moonlight, and a katana glowing blue burst out through the demon's chest. Kaito had come back through a door of his own, an artificial Omenpath built from his spark, Proft's mind magic, Aminatou's fate twisting and the splinter he had carried out, framed in triangles, dragons and hopeful moths. {Get Out} shows that moment. Everyone ran for the light of Ravnica, and Nashi laid his mother's empty scroll down in the passage between worlds and left it there.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "Kaito's blade brought the light of another plane into Duskmourn, and with it, a brief chance to escape.",
                        "Get Out",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "dsk-after-the-house", "After the House",
                    listOf(
                        "The House lost its guests, but it did not lose. In the wreck of the chapel Valgavoth dragged himself down from the ceiling, bleeding, reached into the attic and pulled out a shivering bundle of orange fur. Loot was his now, and Loot knew the way to every world. Then he found Winter cowering where his door had been, promised him freedom of a kind, and melted into the ceiling with him. Some time later, in Proft's quiet office on Ravnica, a door carved with glaring moths began to form in the wall behind him. When he turned around, nothing was there.",
                        "The last tale of the set, It's a Beautiful Day, was told to the reader directly, a walk through the House in the second person in which the reader chooses which way to go. The demon's ambitions reached the next set as well. In [Aetherdrift](aetherdrift) Valgavoth fielded a racing team in the great race across worlds, led by a creature called the Speed Demon and driven by Winter, with Loot caged on the car to find the shortcuts no one else could see.",
                        "The prize was the Aetherspark, and Winter was promised his freedom if he brought it home and the House if he failed. He lost Loot along the way, and it was [Chandra](who/chandra) who brought the little one to the finish line. Winter crawled toward the finish line on a broken leg, and was beaten at the very last second. In that moment a door to Duskmourn opened beneath him and dragged him back inside, howling, and closed behind him.",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Set", "Duskmourn: House of Horror"),
                StorySource("Set", "Aetherdrift"),
                StorySource("Story", "Duskmourn: House of Horror, Episodes 4 to 6 and It's a Beautiful Day, by Mira Grant, Magic Story"),
                StorySource("Story", "Aetherdrift, Episodes 1 to 6, by K. Arsenault Rivera, Magic Story"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Valgavoth"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Marina Vendrell"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "dsk-side-stories", "Side Stories",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "dsk-the-carnival", "Children of Carnival",
                    listOf(
                        "Between the main episodes ran a set of side stories about the people who were born in the House and never knew anything else. The first followed Dawn, a young inventor who lived in the carnival grounds, a fairground in a field in the Mistmoors ringed with cornstalks and evil roses. It had been a safe zone since Duskmourn was first conquered. Dawn built detectors and traps to keep her people alive, and she had never seen a dawn, so she could not have told you how it differed from a sunset.",
                        "Three foragers came back from a run into the House, Rill with a deep gash on her arm from a wickerfolk, Sunset limping on a hurt ankle and cheerfully listing their haul of cheese and jam, and City, the strongest of them, who always came home without a scratch. This time City had news. The House did not need them any more, he said. It could hunt now. He had joined the Cult of Valgavoth, and the carnival's monsters were already through the fence. He offered Dawn a place beside him, and she ran.",
                        "She ran to an attic settlement, where the other survivors did not trust her, and she went on building. Then one night the camp went quiet. City's razorkin had killed everyone in it but her, and City's voice offered her the same choice again, the Valley of Serenity or a death like theirs. He left without harming her. Dawn went out alone, fought a glitch ghost with a device of her own making until its battery died, and was pulled to safety by the Benefactors, who took her in. Rill and Sunset were not so lucky, and turned up again as the cult's prisoners in the main story.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "dsk-keep-them-alive", "Keep Them Alive",
                    listOf(
                        "The second side story was a legend the beasties tell. When the plane's pets were changed by the Ascension, they became beasties, shaggy masked creatures with too many limbs, claws and horns, and at first they moved in a single pack with no idea what they were for. Their leader was Spindlewight, six-legged and three-armed, with spear horns and a blue and green pelt marked with rosettes like moth wings, and a mask made of broken bone and scrap. He went down into the deepest and most dangerous parts of the House looking for the purpose of his kind.",
                        "In a ruined library he found three survivors, a parent and two children, and fought off a cellarspawn to protect them. The parent was mortally wounded anyway, and with their last breath asked him to care for the children. That was the answer he had been looking for. Beasties exist to protect and cherish the survivors, whom they call keep-alives, and Spindlewight carried that purpose back to his people.",
                        "It cost him. Once, out in the House, his mask fell, and one of his keep-alives saw his face and fled in terror. It broke something in him, and he gave up leading the beasties. From that day beasties have never taken off their masks in front of their charges. His bond with the other child held through many adventures until he died, and only his mask came back. Somewhere in the attics is a mural painted by the first two survivors to befriend a beastie and added to by every child and beastie since, showing the world before the House with beasties in it. It is neutral ground for every pack. Spindlewight's story was written after the set was finished, which is why he has no card.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "Sure, I'm pretty small, but Gribble isn't, so I don't get scared.",
                        "Toby, Beastie Befriender",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "dsk-dead-end", "Dead End",
                    listOf(
                        "The third side story went back to the end of the world. Shevara was a scout of the Rotrue elves and the daughter of their king, and the Rotrue Wood was the last free forest on the plane. By then the House, which the elves called simply House, had eaten the cities and the greater sun, and the world lay in a long twilight. Shevara's task was to watch the edges of the wood for any sign that it was coming.",
                        "One day she saw human children playing among the trees with no adults anywhere near them. She knew what that meant. It was the sign that House had reached them at last. She raised the alarm, but her people were slow to believe her, and by the time they did it was too late. House closed over the Rotrue. The forest and the elves inside it were sealed under glass, a conservatory in an endless building, and Shevara understood that the last of the natural world had fallen.",
                        "The Hauntwoods still look like that today, forests trapped indoors and trees holding up the ceilings. It is also why elves, who are native to Duskmourn, are still found inside the walls of the thing that ate their wood. Later the lesser sun was swallowed too, and after that nothing outside was left.",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Story", "Duskmourn: House of Horror, Children of the Carnival, Parts 1 and 2, by Mira Grant, Magic Story"),
                StorySource("Story", "Duskmourn: House of Horror, Keep Them Alive and Dead End, by Mira Grant, Magic Story"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Dawn"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Spindlewight"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Shevara"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "dsk-factions", "Factions",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "dsk-the-cult", "The Cult of Valgavoth",
                    listOf(
                        "The Cult of Valgavoth believes the demon is a blessing. When he devours your fear, they say, he cleanses you of it and protects you from it, and true paradise will come only when he has eaten every fear in existence and made a world where everyone is safe. They call this the Gift of the Threshold. To receive it, cultists climb into green and brown painted cocoons that grow from the walls of their altar room and let the demon feed on them directly. This is the Rite of the Threshold, and cultists who show too much fear or too little faith can be given extra time inside. Some have given so much that hardly anything human is left.",
                        "The cult believes it descends from Valgavoth's original summoner, and it sees itself as the steward of the House. Attendants patch cracks, kill pests and fix damage. Strictors enforce the rules and keep the schedule of rites. Cocooniers run the rituals. Above them stands the seneschal, Victor, the only person who speaks to the demon directly and the only one who knows the path down to the Below. His inner circle, the Imagora, carries out secret orders that even other cultists cannot know, sometimes including the culling of their own.",
                        "The cult prefers not to use violence. It prefers welcome. A survivor stumbling into the Valley of Serenity finds clean beds, safety and friendly faces, and only once they have let their guard down are they given the choice to join of their own free will or be made to. Cultists wear long robes shaped like moth wings and are the best dressed people in the House. Cards like {Valgavoth's Faithful} and {Fanatic of the Harrowing} show their devotion.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "Valgavoth wants our pain, our terror! We cannot join him unless we suffer!",
                        "Fanatic of the Harrowing",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "dsk-the-razorkin", "The Razorkin",
                    listOf(
                        "Razorkin were survivors once. Now they hunt survivors for fun. They live for pain, building elaborate maze traps and inventing new ways to keep their victims alive and suffering for as long as possible, and when there are no survivors to torture they turn on each other or on themselves. They wear masks, carry blades and keep to the razor mazes of the Boilerbilges, which the House has twisted into even more convoluted shapes for them.",
                        "They are not an army and do not act as one, but most of them listen to the Lord of Pain, who appears on the Commander card {The Lord of Pain}. Once a razorkin has chosen a victim it never stops coming, and the only real defence is to kill it first, which does not always take, because razorkin are known to come back from death. {Unstoppable Slasher} is the slasher villain who keeps getting up.",
                        "The razorkin even have a taste for other worlds' horror stories. When the tale of the Meathook Massacre reached them from Innistrad, they were inspired to stage a sequel, and {Meathook Massacre II} is the result, a legendary enchantment named like a horror film. Its partner in the red zone, {The Rollercrusher Ride}, is a carnival ride turned killing machine. Both are mythic, both scale with X, and both read like the title card of a movie nobody should watch.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "dsk-the-survivors", "The Survivors",
                    listOf(
                        "Survivors are what is left of the people who lived on the plane before the House swallowed it, along with the newcomers who keep falling in through its doors. They are smart, tough and resourceful, and most live as nomads, because standing still in the House is how you die. Their numbers shrink all the time as the House picks them off one at a time, and newcomers usually die fast unless they learn quickly or are lucky enough to be found by veterans. They make weapons and tools from whatever they find, from slingshots and bats to flamethrowers and glimmer-powered flashlights, and {Optimistic Scavenger} is one of them.",
                        "A few groups are organised. The House Institute studies the House, recording and analysing its supernatural events to find the one weakness that might end it. It keeps a permanent base in disused operating rooms in the Floodpits, where it dissects whatever creatures it can catch. The Benefactors are hardy nomads who know the House's geography better than anyone, and they mark safe zones and caches of supplies with their flame symbol so other survivors can find them. It was the Benefactors who rescued Dawn.",
                        "The Doorblades are a small and ruthless band who assassinate the monsters of the House. They work closely with the Institute, using its knowledge to guide their strikes and paying for it with corpses and live specimens for its experiments. Their losses are high and so are their kills. Every one of these groups shares one hope, that someone, someday, will find a way out of the House. Nobody has yet.",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Set", "Duskmourn: House of Horror"),
                StorySource("Story", "Planeswalker's Guide to Duskmourn, by Emily Teng, on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Duskmourn"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Victor"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "dsk-horrors-and-hopes", "Horrors and Hopes",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "dsk-nightmares", "Nightmares and Spawn",
                    listOf(
                        "Nightmares are born from the fears of the people inside the House, and on cards they take the names of those fears, from {Fear of Missing Out} to the fear of being watched or abducted or burned alive. A nightmare lives only as long as there is fear to feed it, which is how [Jace](who/jace) was able to unmake one by taking the fear out of the minds trapped inside it. They are enchantment creatures, half real and half dream, and they hunt the people who dreamed them up.",
                        "Cellarspawn are worse. They are Valgavoth's own daydreams given shape, built from the stuff of the House itself, warped and lopsided so that the world bends around them. When one catches a survivor it floods them with pure terror until their mind collapses or their heart bursts, and it treats the result as a delicacy. What is left of a victim is a spindrell, a floating spine holding a scrap of consciousness, steeped in fear forever. A new wave of them spills out after every molt, and almost nothing can stand against one in open combat.",
                        "Over each of the five zones looms an Overlord, a vast Avatar of the House itself. {Overlord of the Mistmoors} sends out swarms of white winged insects, {Overlord of the Floodpits} fills its master.s hand, {Overlord of the Balemurk} digs the dead back up, {Overlord of the Boilerbilges} burns, and {Overlord of the Hauntwoods} grows a land that is every kind of land at once. On cards they arrive slowly with impending, an enchantment first and a monster only later, the way a dread grows before the thing itself appears.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "dsk-ghosts-and-toys", "Ghosts, Wicker, Toys",
                    listOf(
                        "The spirit realm was pushed to the edges of the plane by the Ascension, and the ghosts of people who died outside the House before it came can only get in by squeezing through weak spots, cracks in the walls and reflections. The effort warps them, and they come through flickering and distorted like a bad signal, which is why they are called glitch ghosts. Each is trapped in the feeling it died with. Blue ghosts died confused, some never knowing they had died at all. Red ones died in pain and anger, and black ones died hating and still want revenge. The Floodpits, full of reflecting water and screens, are thick with them.",
                        "Wickerfolk began as people. In the early days of the Ascension, a group of warlocks found a ritual that would make a person untouchable, beyond the reach of fear forever. It worked, in a way. Their bodies became living wood, wicker and bundled sticks, and their minds stayed intact, unable to die and unable to feel. They stand still for hours, mistaken for figures or trees, until someone comes close, and then they push slivers of leaf and branch into the victim's body. {Wildfire Wickerfolk} shows one burning.",
                        "Quickened toys are dolls and playthings that the demon's influence has brought to life with an appetite for death. They do not attack openly. They wait to be picked up by someone who thinks they are harmless, ride along until the whole group has relaxed, and then strike, and afterward they go dormant again until the next survivor picks them up. The only way to stop one is to destroy all of it, because any piece left, even a single limb, keeps moving and keeps hunting. {Twitching Doll} and {Arabella, Abandoned Doll} are two of them.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "dsk-glimmers-beasties", "Glimmers and Beasties",
                    listOf(
                        "Not everything in the House is out to kill you. Glimmers are light made out of a survivor's hope and persistence, glowing golden shapes of a treasured toy, a beloved pet, a friend, whatever a person holds on to so as not to give up. They ward off Valgavoth's malice and help keep a survivor from falling under the House's sway, and on cards they are always enchantment creatures. The Wanderer's glimmer was a tiny Kyodai. A glimmer goes out when its person dies or loses hope, and most long-time survivors have lost theirs. Winter lost his long ago.",
                        "The five rare Enduring glimmers, {Enduring Innocence}, {Enduring Curiosity}, {Enduring Tenacity}, {Enduring Courage} and {Enduring Vitality}, are a sheep, a cat, a snake, a dog and an elk, and they show what a glimmer is about. When one dies it comes back to the battlefield as an enchantment that is no longer a creature. Hope can be knocked down, but it does not entirely leave. {Inquisitive Glimmer} and the little Glimmer tokens of the set carry the same light.",
                        "Beasties are the plane's old pets, changed by the Ascension. They begin small, spin silk cocoons, and come out as big shaggy creatures with too many limbs, claws, horns and fangs. They cannot always speak, but they understand everything said to them, and although they are predators they never eat thinking creatures. Their faces are dried flesh pulled tight, and the masks they wear hide that from the survivors they protect, because their deepest fear is that a keep-alive will see them and run. Beasties are the only creatures known to stand a chance against cellarspawn. {Toby, Beastie Befriender} and {Bedhead Beastie} show the bond from both sides.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "Glimmers are physical manifestations of survivors' hope and courage. Their presence is one of the few things that can protect from Valgavoth's malevolent influence.",
                        "Inquisitive Glimmer",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Set", "Duskmourn: House of Horror"),
                StorySource("Story", "Planeswalker's Guide to Duskmourn, by Emily Teng, on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Glimmer"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Toy"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Beast"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "dsk-characters", "Characters",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "dsk-the-rescuers", "The Rescuers",
                    listOf(
                        "Five came through the cherry door, and each has a legendary card. {Niko, Light of Hope} is the shardmage of Theros, a former planeswalker still angry about the spark they lost, who throws shards of light that never miss and who was the one Winter guided and betrayed. {Tyvar, the Pummeler} is the Kaldheim elf prince who laughs through everything and takes on the substance of what he touches, and in the House he learned to wear its own flesh as a disguise. {Zimone, All-Questioning} is the Strixhaven prodigy who could hardly bear to leave the library of a dead world, and who first understood what Marina's diary meant.",
                        "{Kaito, Bane of Nightmares} is the ninja planeswalker of Kamigawa, one of the few walkers who kept his spark, who was thrown out of the House and came back through a door he built himself. His card carries ninjutsu, a mechanic borrowed from his home plane. {The Wandering Rescuer} is the Wanderer, emperor of Kamigawa, who lost her spark and found peace in it, and who dueled Valgavoth on his own altar.",
                        "The boy they came for is {Nashi, Searcher in the Dark}, Tamiyo's adopted son, who followed his mother's voice into the House and in the end chose to let her go. Their ally on Ravnica, Aminatou, who twisted fate to give each of them one escape from death, got her own card in the Commander decks, {Aminatou, Veil Piercer}. The second Zimone card, {Zimone, Mystery Unraveler}, leads one of those decks too.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "dsk-the-natives", "The Natives",
                    listOf(
                        "{Winter, Misanthropic Guide} is the survivor who knows the House better than almost anyone and will sell what he knows to anyone, for a price. He came in through a door as a teenager with his best friend, and in time he gave that friend to Valgavoth as the first of four lives. He is bitter and cynical, he keeps everyone at arm's length with dry jokes, and he has been thrown out of more than one survivor group. He has no magic at all, only a slingshot and his wits. He claimed to have lived in the House most of his life, then admitted he was not from there, and his own author has hinted that not everything he says is true.",
                        "{Marina Vendrell} is the girl who made all of this. She lives in a bubble of unreality where the House looks like a well-kept home in soft light, and she wanders its halls reading old books and walking in fields of flowers. Monsters stay away from her, and the calm she leaves behind lingers long enough for other survivors to rest. Some believe she alone could defeat Valgavoth. Nobody dares to wake her to the truth, because when she is disturbed, hordes of cellarspawn come to kill everyone near her so that she can go on not knowing.",
                        "{Victor, Valgavoth's Seneschal} leads the cult and calls himself the First Child of the All-Devouring Father. He is small, soft-spoken and wears spectacles clouded with scratches, and he carries a leather book laced with sedative moth powder. He has gone through the Rite of the Threshold more than three dozen times, more than anyone in the House's memory, and it is not clear there is anything of the man left. Whether he survived the battle in the chapel is not known.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "dsk-the-demon", "The Demon",
                    listOf(
                        "{Valgavoth, Terror Eater} is the House. He is an elder demon in the shape of an enormous moth, with faceted eyes, feathered antennae, a mouth of teeth like broken needles and glass, and wings that have grown into the walls. Every room is part of his body, every moth is one of his eyes, and the floors and walls are his flesh. He feeds on fear, and on the memories and feelings of the people he catches. His cult calls him the Devouring Father. Marina called him Val.",
                        "He is not a mindless beast. He is patient, cunning and cruel, and he treats fear as an art, shaping the House to fit the worst fears of each person inside it. One of his first acts after the Ascension was to kill most of the plane's other demons and break the rest, so that none could challenge him. He can bend time inside the House, keep planeswalkers from entering it, and open doors to other worlds. He wants to open them on every world there is, and with Loot he finally had a way to find them.",
                        "On his card he is a giant flier who exiles everything his enemies lose and lets you play it as your own by paying life. In the Commander decks he leads a second time as {Valgavoth, Harrower of Souls}, a demon who feeds on every point of life the other players lose. He was not a moth on his type line in the end, because Elder Demon Insect did not fit, and the designers thought Elder was the better word.",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Set", "Duskmourn: House of Horror"),
                StorySource("Story", "The Legends of Duskmourn: House of Horror, by Emily Teng, on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Valgavoth"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Winter"),
                StorySource("Reference", "Scryfall"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "dsk-mechanics", "Mechanics",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "dsk-rooms-and-eerie", "Rooms and Eerie",
                    listOf(
                        "Duskmourn is a set about enchantments, and its biggest new idea is the Room, a new enchantment type. A Room card is printed sideways like a split card, with two doors, each with its own name, mana cost and effect. When you cast it you choose one door and pay for that one, and the Room enters with that door unlocked and the other one locked. Later, whenever you could cast a sorcery, you can pay the other door's cost to unlock it, and then the Room does both things. The key art of the set, {Mirror Room}, is one of the five mythic Rooms.",
                        "Rooms play like walking deeper into the House. You open one door now and come back for the next when you can afford it. A Room is a single permanent, so destroying it shuts both doors at once, and it counts as one enchantment entering however many doors it has. Some Rooms put their cheap effect in front and their payoff behind, and some are built so that the order matters, so that a player chooses which fear to face first.",
                        "Rooms come with a new ability word, eerie. An eerie ability triggers whenever an enchantment enters under your control and also whenever you fully unlock a Room, which means opening the last locked door. It is a close cousin of constellation from Theros, with the extra twist that finishing a Room counts too. The blue and white and the blue and black draft decks were both built on it.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "dsk-impending-manifest", "Impending and Dread",
                    listOf(
                        "Impending appears only on the five Overlords. Each has a normal mana cost and a cheaper impending cost with a number. If you cast it for the impending cost, it enters with that many time counters, and while it has any it is not a creature, only an enchantment that does its work. At the end of each of your turns one counter comes off, and when the last is gone the Overlord wakes as a full creature. Its main ability triggers when it enters or attacks, so a cheap Overlord does its thing at once and then looms for a few turns before it arrives in person.",
                        "Manifest dread brings back the old manifest mechanic from Fate Reforged with a twist. You look at the top two cards of your library, put one of them onto the battlefield face down as a 2/2 creature, and put the other into your graveyard. If the face-down card is a creature card, you can turn it face up at any time by paying its mana cost. It is the thing in the dark you cannot quite see, and it is the heart of the green and blue draft deck. There is even a card simply called {Manifest Dread}.",
                        "Because the second card goes into the graveyard, manifest dread also feeds delirium, so the two mechanics work hand in hand. Face-down creatures in this set do not have to stay hidden for long. Many cards care about turning them face up, and {Zimone, Mystery Unraveler} in the Commander decks builds a whole strategy around lands that bring more of them out.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "dsk-survival-delirium", "Survival and Delirium",
                    listOf(
                        "Survival is a new ability word. A survival ability triggers at the beginning of your second main phase if that creature is tapped. In practice that means it attacked and lived through combat, so it survived the horror that came for it. It is the theme of the green and white draft deck, and it rewards attacking with creatures that are hard to kill.",
                        "Delirium returns from Shadows over Innistrad. A delirium ability turns on when there are four or more card types among the cards in your graveyard. Duskmourn is full of the types that help, enchantment creatures, artifact creatures, Rooms and lands among them, and manifest dread throws a card into the graveyard every time it is used. The red and green deck is delirium aggro and the black and green deck is a slow delirium grind, and the Commander deck led by {Winter, Cynical Opportunist} is built on it.",
                        "The other draft decks round out the House. Black and red sacrifices its own creatures. White and black reanimates big monsters. Blue and red controls the game with Rooms. Red and white swarms with small creatures of power two or less. The set also introduced the Glimmer and Toy creature types, printed the first card that is not a token with the Survivor creature type, and gave the Doctor, Balloon and Clown types their first appearances outside Universes Beyond and the joke sets.",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Set", "Duskmourn: House of Horror"),
                StorySource("Story", "Duskmourn: House of Horror Mechanics, by Matt Tabak, on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Room"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Impending"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Manifest dread"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "dsk-cards", "Cards Worth Knowing",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "dsk-the-cycles", "The Big Cycles",
                    listOf(
                        "The mythic headliners are the five Overlords, enchantment creatures in the mold of the old Titans, with a strong effect each time they enter or attack. {Overlord of the Floodpits} and {Overlord of the Hauntwoods} found homes in competitive decks, and {Overlord of the Balemurk} is a natural engine for graveyard strategies. Five mythic Rooms join them, {Dollmaker's Shop}, {Mirror Room}, {Funeral Room}, {Charred Foyer} and {Walk-In Closet}, each with a full-art version.",
                        "The Enduring glimmers are the rare cycle that Commander players went after first. {Enduring Curiosity} turns any creature deck into a card-drawing engine, {Enduring Vitality} gives every creature the ability to tap for mana, and {Enduring Tenacity} drains an opponent whenever you gain life. Because they come back as plain enchantments when they die, they are very hard to remove for good.",
                        "The set also brought back the Leylines, enchantments that can start the game on the battlefield if they are in your opening hand. {Leyline of the Void} returned as a reprint, joined by four new ones, among them {Leyline of Resonance}. At the uncommon level, ten unlucky dual lands enter tapped unless a player has thirteen or less life, a nod to the fear of the number thirteen, and each one's flavor text is an urban legend that begins with They say. Five rare verges complete the mana.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "dsk-legends-and-icons", "Legends and Icons",
                    listOf(
                        "The legends of Duskmourn cover every corner of the House. {Valgavoth, Terror Eater} is the demon himself. {Kaito, Bane of Nightmares} is the planeswalker who came back with a blade of light. {Marina Vendrell} is a five-color legend who pulls every enchantment from the top seven cards of your library into your hand and can lock and unlock the doors of your Rooms. {Arabella, Abandoned Doll} is a quickened toy, {Rip, Spawn Hunter} hunts monsters, {The Swarmweaver} commands swarms of insects, and {The Jolly Balloon Man} is exactly as unsettling as his name.",
                        "Some cards are worth knowing for the story they hold. {Demonic Counsel} shows Valgavoth whispering to Marina, {Enter the Enigma} shows the Wanderer crossing the threshold, {Shardmage's Rescue} shows Niko saving Nashi, {Betrayer's Bargain} shows Winter's deal, and {Get Out} shows Kaito's blade bursting through the demon. These are the set's story spotlight cards, the moments the story was built around.",
                        "Others are iconic as horror. {Unstoppable Slasher} is the killer who keeps getting up. {Chainsaw} is exactly what it sounds like, and it came with a new kind of counter, the rev counter. {Living Phone} rings with something on the other end. {Valgavoth's Lair} is an enchantment land, only the second ever printed, and {Abhorrent Oculus} became a staple of competitive decks soon after release.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "dsk-treatments", "Treatments and Extras",
                    listOf(
                        "Duskmourn had 276 cards in the main set, sold in Play Boosters and Collector Boosters, with alternate treatments that played on horror films. The Paranormal showcase gave cards a modernist frame. The Double Exposure showcase showed the legends as if in two overlapping photographs, and in textured foil in Collector Boosters. Lurking Evil cards reused a card's original art but slipped a monster in somewhere, and Mirror Monster borderless cards showed the monster only in a reflection. Five full-art Manor basic lands showed the House's rooms.",
                        "It was also the first set with Japan Showcase cards, ten Overlords and Enduring glimmers redrawn by Japanese artists in a style paying homage to Japanese hobby shops, all printed at mythic and found only in Collector Boosters, some in a new fractured foil. Ten Special Guests cards appeared in Play Boosters, including {Soul Warden} and {Damnation}, the second with Valgavoth in its art.",
                        "A special Nightmare Bundle followed a few weeks after release, with foil Manor lands, double-sided movie posters, a glow-in-the-dark life counter and promo cards drawn as horror film posters. The set also revived Welcome Decks for new players, unseen since Core Set 2019, and held a Trick or Treat event the week of Halloween in which every enchantment that entered made a Treasure or a Food.",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Set", "Duskmourn: House of Horror"),
                StorySource("Story", "Collecting Duskmourn, by Mike Turian and Jubilee Finnegan, on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Duskmourn: House of Horror"),
                StorySource("Reference", "Scryfall"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "dsk-commander", "Commander Decks",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "dsk-four-decks", "The Four Decks",
                    listOf(
                        "Four Commander decks came out alongside the set on the same day, under the code DSC, each with ten new cards. Miracle Worker is white, blue and black, led by {Aminatou, Veil Piercer}, who gives the enchantments in your hand miracle so they can be cast cheaply when drawn. Death Toll is black and green, led by {Winter, Cynical Opportunist}, and it fills the graveyard for delirium and turns it into an army, with cards like {Rendmaw, Creaking Nest} beside him.",
                        "Endless Punishment is black and red, led by {Valgavoth, Harrower of Souls}, and it hurts everyone at the table all the time, a group slug deck that grows stronger with every point of life the other players lose. Its new cards include {The Lord of Pain} and {Persistent Constrictor}. The fourth deck, Jump Scare, is green and blue, led by {Zimone, Mystery Unraveler}, and it mixes landfall with manifest dread, with {Kianne, Corrupted Memory} among its new cards.",
                        "Each deck brought new Rooms, such as {Polluted Cistern}, {Experimental Lab}, {Secret Arcade} and {Cramped Vents}, along with new nightmares, investigators and beasties, and plenty of reprints with fresh art suited to a haunted house. New cards like {The Master of Keys} and {Phenomenon Investigators} gave enchantment decks fresh tools well beyond these four lists.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "dsk-archenemy", "Archenemy",
                    listOf(
                        "The Duskmourn decks brought back Archenemy, the format in which one player is a villain with a stack of scheme cards and everyone else plays together against them. Each deck came with ten new schemes, and every one is spoken by Valgavoth, with names like {I Am Duskmourn}, {My Wings Enfold All} and {Your Nightmares Are Delicious}. Any of the four decks can play the villain.",
                        "The rules were reworked for Commander. In the old version the Archenemy had forty life and each hero twenty, which made it best to gang up on a single hero and knock them out early, leaving that player watching for the rest of the game. In the new version the Archenemy has sixty life and the whole hero team shares sixty between them, so they win or lose together. A deck needs only ten schemes to play.",
                        "Archenemy is entirely optional, and the decks work perfectly well as ordinary Commander decks without it. But it suits the theme better than anything else could, because in the House there is always one enemy above everyone, and the only hope is to face it together. The schemes even carry the demon's voice, so a player running them gets to speak as the House itself.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "I am infinite. All that you behold, even the stars above, are my domain.",
                        "I Am Duskmourn",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Set", "Duskmourn: House of Horror Commander"),
                StorySource("Story", "Evolving Archenemy, by Gavin Verhey, on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Duskmourn: House of Horror/Commander decks"),
                StorySource("Reference", "Scryfall"),
            ),
        ),
    ),
)
